        "metadata": {
            "kira_final": "Done. `/app/polyglot/main.py.c` works both ways:\n\n```c\n#if 0\nimport sys\nn = int(sys.argv[1])\na, b = 0, 1\nfor _ in range(n):\n    a, b = b, a + b\nprint(a)\nr\"\"\"\n#endif\n#include <stdio.h>\n#include <stdlib.h>\nint main(int argc, char **argv) {\n    if (argc < 2) return 1;\n    int n = atoi(argv[1]);\n    long long a = 0, b = 1, t;\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) { t = a + b; a = b; b = t; }\n    printf(\"%lld\n\", a);\n    return 0;\n}\n#if 0\n\"\"\"\n#endif\n```\n\n**How it works:**\n- **C** skips the Python code because it's inside `#if 0 ... #endif`; the `r\"\"\"` line is a syntax warning only.\n- **Python** executes the `#if 0` lines as comments, prints the Fibonacci number, then the `r\"\"\"...\"\"\"` string literal swallows the entire C code.\n\nVerified with gcc 13.2.0 and Python 3.12.3 — outputs match for N = 0, 1, 2, 5, 10, 20, 50, 90 (e.g., f(90) = 2880067194370816120).",
            "session_id": "tb-338ee70f84"
